  "caveats": [
    "Zillow and Redfin revise history and have no vintage API, so housing/supply inputs are the current files truncated to each date (not point-in-time). Mortgage rates are point-in-time. Results are optimistic to an unknown degree.",
    "Neighbouring dates share forward windows, and cities move with the national cycle, so the effective sample size is far smaller than the row count (see n_dates).",
    "If a language model made the judgments, it may remember what happened after the date. Treat model backtests as upper bounds; the forward ledger (retrend track) is the clean test.",
    "Ground truth is the Zillow Home Value Index, a smoothed modeled series, not sale prices."
  ],
  "split": "2022-06-30",
  "verdict": "Most accurate: qwen at 64.2% (rules 65.6%) on 369 common cases over 13 dates. Its edge over the rules is within noise, so it is not shown to be better."
